# Rust
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Cargo.lock is deliberately NOT ignored. charkit ships a binary, and a
# committed lockfile is what makes `cargo install --locked` and the release
# build reproducible.

# charkit's own workspace state. Gitignored by design — docs/PLAN.md §4.2:
# ".char/ holds nothing reclaimable", precisely because a workspace directory
# gets deleted without char's involvement. Machine-global state lives in
# ~/.char/char.db, outside any repo.
.char/

# .claude/ is deliberately NOT ignored. Phase 1 ships the PreToolUse
# clean-room hook in .claude/settings.json (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md §2.7) and it
# only enforces anything if it is committed.
#
# Its inputs are, though. Both name the private repo this one was built away
# from — a path and a name belonging to whoever set them, not to this repo. The
# hook and the grep are committed; what they point at is not.
.claude/clean-room.local
.claude/contamination.local

# Agent run logs. They record the raw prompt that started a run and every
# iteration's transcript, so they hold whatever was typed — including the
# source repo's name, which is the one string this repo exists to not carry.
# Ignored here rather than in .git/info/exclude so the rule survives a clone.
.gnhf/

# Review artifacts. Scratch HTML built for a browser review loop, carrying
# whatever the session was about — same reasoning as .gnhf/ above.
.lavish/

# macOS
.DS_Store

# Editors
*.swp
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Agent worktrees. Transient checkouts a subagent works in; they are git repos
# of their own and must never be committed into this one.
.claude/worktrees/
